<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<PermissionSet xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        This comment sits INSIDE the root element deliberately - a comment above the root breaks
        `sf` at source conversion with "unable to find matching parent xml file".
        Disposition_Deal_Driver / Broker_Protection_Access / DPEG_Admin_Access are the precedents.
        Added 2026-08-10 by the permission set cleanup (KEEP item K4): this set already carried the
        full rationale in its disposition TWIN and only a one-line description here, which made the
        acquisitions half look like the trivial one-field set it is safe to fold away. It is not.

        🔴 DO NOT FOLD THIS SET INTO DPEG_Acquisition_Edit, AND DO NOT DELETE IT AS "TRIVIAL".
        This is a LAYER-5 AUTHORIZATION FLAG set: exactly one fieldPermissions entry, on a User
        field, read by a two-factor gate. It is the twin of Disposition_Deal_Driver, whose own
        comment argues the same case for the sell side.

        THE GATE IS TWO-FACTOR (ARCHITECTURE.md section 2, "Opportunity deal-action gate is
        TWO-FACTOR"). OpportunityActionPermissionService.hasDealActionAccess() requires BOTH:
          (a) CAPABILITY    - assignment of a set granting FLS read on User.Deal_Driver__c, i.e.
                              THIS set. UserSelector reads the field WITH USER_MODE, and USER_MODE
                              THROWS rather than degrades, so an unassigned user makes the query
                              throw and the service converts that throw to false.
          (b) AUTHORIZATION - Deal_Driver__c = true on the running user's OWN User record.
        The six deployed Dynamic Actions visibility rules on Opportunity_Record_Page ask the
        identical question ({!$User.Deal_Driver__c} EQUAL true), so the button chrome and the
        server-side assertion agree by construction.

        THREE WAYS TO BREAK IT, ALL OF WHICH LOOK LIKE TIDYING:
          1. Moving this fieldPermission into DPEG_Acquisition_Edit (or into the new
             Opportunity_Stage_Actions_Access capability set) hands factor (a) to that set's
             ENTIRE population as a side effect of editing acquisitions. The gate becomes
             one-factor for everyone who holds it.
          2. Replacing the gate with a permission-set MEMBERSHIP check does the same thing in
             code: a holder of this set with Deal_Driver__c = false is DENIED today and would be
             GRANTED by a membership check.
             OpportunityActionPermissionServiceTest.hasDealActionAccess_membershipWithoutTheFlag_isStillDenied
             exists to go red if anyone tries. Do not modify that test.
          3. Deleting this set as a "trivial one-field set" denies EVERY deal driver - silently.
             The USER_MODE read throws, the service returns false, and the buttons simply stop
             working. No error surfaces anywhere.

        THE CAPABILITY HALF NOW LIVES IN Opportunity_Stage_Actions_Access (added 2026-08-10).
        The two sets are the two factors of one gate and must never be merged, in either
        direction. That file carries the mirror of this note.

        ASSIGNMENT IS AN ORG ACTION, NOT DEPLOYABLE METADATA, and this set is assigned DIRECTLY
        rather than through DPEG_Junior_Analyst_PSG - deliberately, because its population is
        narrower than any group's. Deploying it grants nobody anything.

        ⚠ AN ADMIN SMOKE TEST PROVES NOTHING ABOUT THIS GATE. A bare System Administrator has no
        FLS on User.Deal_Driver__c either - Metadata-API-deployed custom fields arrive with no
        field permissions for ANY profile, System Administrator included, and Modify All Data is
        an OBJECT permission that confers no FLS. That is precisely why the service checks Modify
        All Data BEFORE it reads the flag; reversing those two statements locks out every
        administrator. Acceptance-test as a real deal-driver persona.
    -->
    <description>Marks the &quot;Junior&quot; deal-driver: grants read on User.Deal_Driver__c so the deal-driving quick actions (stage advances; Submit for Approval) show only for holders whose Deal_Driver__c = true. Not assigned to approvers.</description>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>false</editable>
        <field>User.Deal_Driver__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <hasActivationRequired>false</hasActivationRequired>
    <label>Acquisition Deal Driver</label>
</PermissionSet>
